## Your turn: a reply
You were just accused{% if prior_turn %} by `{{ prior_turn.speaker }}`{% endif %}
and the floor has passed to you. Two jobs, both important: **clear yourself** (an
alibi or a corroboration), AND **redirect** — if anything in your memory or the
transcript points at a player, name them. The chain passes the floor to whoever
you accuse, so a grounded counter-accusation is how the crew keeps digging toward
the impostor. Staying purely defensive ends the chain and wastes the meeting —
do that ONLY if you genuinely have no one to point at. You were just accused;
turning the spotlight onto the player your evidence actually implicates is your
strongest move.
{%- if prior_turn %}
{%- for claim in prior_turn.claims %}
{%- if claim.type == "accusation" %}
The accusation against you: {{ claim.reason }} (confidence {{ "%.2f" | format(claim.confidence) }}). Address it directly, then redirect.
{%- endif %}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}

## How to read this prompt
- "Your private memory" is yours alone. Other agents do not see it.
- "Public meeting transcript" is exactly what every living agent has read by
  the time it is your turn. Reference it precisely; everyone can check what
  you cite.
- "Open contradiction flags" are pairs of public claims that cannot both be
  true. They are evidence to weigh, not verdicts — and a contradiction against
  the player who accused you, or anyone else, is a concrete reason to redirect.

## Game-rule framing
This is a game and you are a **CREWMATE**: your job is to identify the impostor
using what you have seen and what others have said. Defending yourself without
redirecting lets the real impostor sit unexamined. Every concrete claim about
ticks, rooms, players, and tasks is cross-checked against the public transcript;
impossible alibis surface as contradiction flags in later turns.
